    "captions": [
      { "t": 0.0, "text": "Night of 30 June 1520. Moctezuma is dead and the city has risen. Cortés decides to slip out in the dark and rain along the Tlacopan causeway, the shortest road to the mainland." },
      { "t": 0.5, "text": "The column carries a portable wooden bridge to span the gaps where the Mexica have removed the causeway bridges. Many soldiers are weighed down with gold." },
      { "t": 0.9, "text": "They are spotted. Drums sound from the Great Temple, and canoes pour out across the lake." },
      { "t": 1.4, "text": "The bridge jams fast in the first gap and cannot be carried forward. Behind it the column packs together." },
      { "t": 2.0, "text": "Attacked from canoes on both sides, men are dragged into the water. The next gaps fill with bodies, horses, baggage and gold until survivors can cross on top of them." },
      { "t": 3.0, "text": "The rearguard under Alvarado and the Tlaxcalans suffer worst. Legend says Alvarado vaulted a gap on his lance; the spot is still called Puente de Alvarado." },
      { "t": 4.2, "text": "At Popotla the survivors regroup. Spanish tradition says Cortés sat under a tree and wept." },
      { "t": 5.0, "text": "Perhaps half or more of the Spaniards, and thousands of Tlaxcalan allies, were killed, captured or drowned. The Spaniards called it the Noche Triste, the sad night." }
    ]
  },

