    "captions": [
      { "t": 0.0, "text": "May 1521. Cortés has around 900 Spaniards, 13 brigantines and, by his own account, tens of thousands of Indigenous allies, above all Tlaxcalans." },
      { "t": 0.6, "text": "26 May: Alvarado and Olid cut the aqueduct from the springs of Chapultepec. The city of perhaps 200,000 people now depends on brackish lake water." },
      { "t": 0.9, "text": "The brigantines smash the Mexica canoe fleet. From now on Cortés controls the lake." },
      { "t": 1.4, "text": "Three divisions hold the causeways: Alvarado at Tlacopan, Olid at Coyoacán, Sandoval at Iztapalapa. Sandoval then marches round to Tepeyac to close the last road out." },
      { "t": 3.6, "text": "Each day the attackers fight their way in along the causeways; each night the defenders reopen the breaches. Cortés begins demolishing the city house by house to fill the canals." },
      { "t": 5.3, "text": "30 June: an assault toward the Tlatelolco market is ambushed. Cortés is nearly captured; dozens of Spaniards are taken and sacrificed in sight of their comrades." },
      { "t": 7.0, "text": "Hunger and disease ravage the city. More towns join Cortés as Mexica power collapses." },
      { "t": 9.0, "text": "The defenders are forced back into Tlatelolco, the market twin city to the north." },
      { "t": 11.0, "text": "Almost the whole city is in ruins, its canals full of the dead." },
      { "t": 11.8, "text": "13 August 1521: Cuauhtémoc tries to escape by canoe and is captured by a brigantine. The siege is over." }
    ]
